<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Neris</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Viliya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Neris is a river of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, about 510 kilometres long, known in Belarus as the Viliya. It rises in Belarus, flows through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a> and joins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neman">Neman</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaunas">Kaunas</a>. Its valley formed part of the heartland of the early Lithuanian state, and the capital Vilnius grew up at its confluence with the Vilnia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/neris.jpg" alt="Neris" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mindaur · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Neris_Vilnius_20201031.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
